WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.579
Why logs stop being enough.

2
00:00:01.859 --> 00:00:08.365
In Studio 1 a request was one model call, and one log line per call told you most of what you needed.

3
00:00:08.645 --> 00:00:10.992
The systems you’ve built since are not like that.

4
00:00:11.272 --> 00:00:16.307
A RAG answer is retrieval, maybe reranking, a model call, and a citation check.

5
00:00:16.587 --> 00:00:21.963
An agent is a loop: model call, tool call, model call, tool call, until it stops.

6
00:00:22.243 --> 00:00:28.835
A single user request can become a dozen operations across your code, a model provider, and a few tools.

7
00:00:29.115 --> 00:00:36.347
When that request is slow, wrong, or expensive, the question you need answered is about the request, not any single call.

8
00:00:36.627 --> 00:00:38.013
Which step took the time?

9
00:00:38.293 --> 00:00:40.128
Which tool returned the bad data?

10
00:00:40.408 --> 00:00:43.779
How many model calls did the agent make, and why so many?

11
00:00:44.059 --> 00:00:54.789
Separate log lines can hold all the facts and still make these questions slow to answer, because nothing ties the lines of one request together or says which step caused which.

12
00:00:55.069 --> 00:00:58.440
Tracing is the standard answer from distributed systems.

13
00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:08.149
Every request gets an identifier, every step records its start, end, and outcome under that identifier, and each step points to the step that called it.

14
00:01:08.429 --> 00:01:11.352
The result is a tree you can read like a timeline.

15
00:01:11.632 --> 00:01:20.720
For LLM systems tracing has a second payoff: the traces you collect are the raw material for error analysis and evals (Studio 3).

16
00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:30.621
Hamel Husain and Shreya Shankar’s evals FAQ puts reading real traces at the center of error analysis, and you can only read what you recorded.
